Chapter 3: Making Decisions in C#

1. Decision Structures and the if Statement

A decision structure is used to make a decision in a program. It executes a statement when a condition is true.

Example:

if (age >= 18)
{
    MessageBox.Show("You are an adult");
}

2. Relational Operators

Relational operators are used to compare two values.

- ">" Greater than
- "<" Less than
- ">=" Greater than or equal to
- "<=" Less than or equal to
- "==" Equal to
- "!=" Not equal to

3. The if-else Statement

The "if-else" statement is used when there are two possible results. If the condition is true, the "if" block runs. Otherwise, the "else" block runs.

Example:

if (mark >= 50)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Pass");
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Fail");
}

4. Nested Decision Structures

A nested decision structure means placing one "if" statement inside another "if" statement. It is used to check more than one condition.

Example:

if (age >= 18)
{
    if (age <= 60)
    {
        MessageBox.Show("Allowed");
    }
}

5. The if-else-if Statement

This statement is used to check several conditions. The program executes the block for the first condition that is true.

Example:

if (mark >= 80)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Grade A");
}
else if (mark >= 60)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Grade B");
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Grade C");
}

6. Logical Operators

Logical operators are used to combine or reverse conditions.

- "&&" (AND): Both conditions must be true.
- "||" (OR): At least one condition must be true.
- "!" (NOT): Reverses the result of a condition.

Example:

if (age >= 18 && age <= 30)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Age accepted");
}

7. bool Variables and Flags

A "bool" variable stores only two values: "true" or "false".

A flag is a variable used to indicate whether a particular condition exists.

Example:

bool isPassed = true;

8. Comparing Strings

Strings can be compared using the "==" and "!=" operators. The "String.Compare()" method can also be used to compare two strings.

Example:

if (name1 == name2)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Names are equal");
}

9. The TryParse Method

"TryParse()" is used to convert input into a numeric value without causing a conversion exception when the input is invalid.

Common methods are:

- "int.TryParse()"
- "double.TryParse()"
- "decimal.TryParse()"

Example:

int age;

if (int.TryParse(textBox1.Text, out age))
{
    MessageBox.Show(age.ToString());
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Enter a valid number");
}

10. Input Validation

Input validation means checking whether the data entered by the user is valid before using it in a program.

For example, a test score should be between 0 and 100.

if (score >= 0 && score <= 100)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Valid score");
}

11. Radio Buttons and Check Boxes

RadioButton: Allows the user to select one option from a group of choices.

CheckBox: Allows the user to select or deselect an option. Multiple check boxes can be selected.

The "Checked" property is used to check whether an option is selected.

Example:

if (radioButton1.Checked)
{
    MessageBox.Show("Option selected");
}

12. The CheckedChanged Event

The "CheckedChanged" event occurs when the "Checked" property of a RadioButton or CheckBox changes. It allows the program to respond to a change in selection.

13. The switch Statement

The "switch" statement selects a block of code based on the value of an expression. It can be used instead of several "if-else-if" statements.

Example:

switch (day)
{
    case 1:
        MessageBox.Show("Monday");
        break;

    case 2:
        MessageBox.Show("Tuesday");
        break;

    default:
        MessageBox.Show("Invalid day");
        break;
}

The "break" statement ends the current case. The "default" section runs when no case matches.

14. Introduction to List Boxes

A ListBox displays a list of items and allows the user to select an item.

Common properties and methods:

- "Text": Gets the displayed text of the selected item.
- "Items": Contains the items in the list.
- "Items.Count": Returns the number of items.
- "SelectedItem": Gets the selected item.
- "SelectedIndex": Gets the index of the selected item. The first item has index "0".
- "Items.Add()": Adds an item.
- "Items.Remove()": Removes a specific item.
- "Items.RemoveAt()": Removes an item by index.
- "Items.Clear()": Removes all items.

Example:

listBox1.Items.Add("Monday");
listBox1.Items.Add("Tuesday");

To remove an item:

listBox1.Items.Remove("Monday");

To check whether an item is selected:

if (listBox1.SelectedIndex != -1)
{
    MessageBox.Show(listBox1.SelectedItem.ToString());
}

Conclusion

Decision structures help a C# program make choices based on conditions. The "if", "if-else", and "switch" statements are used to control program execution. Logical operators, input validation, TryParse, RadioButtons, CheckBoxes, and ListBoxes help make Windows Forms applications more useful and reliable.
